  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and pleasant weather \nlinger through the weekend.\n\nFair weather cumulus today provides some pockets of shade today\nas temperatures peak in the upper 70s to low 80s, seasonable \nfor late summer in southern New England. Seabreezes will keep \nthe coastline slightly cooler before kicking out around sunset. \nThere remains a low chance for isolated showers, mainly across \neastern and southeastern MA, late this afternoon as a shortwave \nmoves through the region. If any showers do form, they are \nlikely to be brief with light rain as opposed to heavier \ndownpours.\n\nMuch like last night, skies will clear and winds will calm, \nallowing for good radiational cooling and chances for valley fog\nto develop. Temperatures tonight likely bottom out in the low \nto mid 50s, near 60 for urban areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return late Sunday into Monday, \nfollowed by quieter weather through late-week. Unsettled weather\nreturns late-week.\n\nA mid-level shortwave trough moves through the flow late Sunday\ninto Monday with a surface low developing and tracking \noffshore. This will support our next shot at rainfall. Most of \nSunday is likely to stay dry with most model guidance showing \narrival of showers Sunday night-early Monday AM. Batches of rain\ncontinue through the day Monday. There is still uncertainty in \nthe details of this system, mainly with respect to the \ndevelopment/track of a surface low pressure tracking near \nsouthern New England and how it interacts with the low pressure \ntracking across New England. This will lead to lower confidence \nin expected rain amounts with this system. A stronger, closer \npositioned surface low to southern New England would provide \nmore forcing for more moderate to heavy rainfall. With PWATs \n1.5-1.8\" and marginal instability, embedded showers/storms with \nheavier rain rates are supported, especially where there will be\nthat additional forcing with the surface low. In terms of flash\nflooding potential, mid-level winds will be strong enough to \nkeep any heavier showers or storms moving reducing widespread \nflash flood threat. At this point, given the potential for some \nheavier showers, can't rule out isolated instances of flash \nflood impacts in poor drainage/urban areas. This will become \nmore clear as we get a better idea on the finer details of the \nsystem. The WPC ERO as a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) which \naligns well with the potential for localized heavier downpours. \nFor total amounts, ensemble guidance shows a range of 0.25\" to \n0.60\" across southern New England though locally heavier amounts\npossible, especially beneath any downpours. Ensemble \nprobabilities for greater than 1.0\" are lower (< 30%), but do \nshow the lower end potential for higher amounts if the surface \nlow were to track closer to the south coast of southern New \nEngland. \n\nA cold front moves through the region by Tuesday with a drier \nairmass advecting into southern New England behind it. \nConditions remain generally dry Tuesday and Wednesday with zonal\nflow aloft. Temperatures will likely be in seasonable ranges in\nthe upper 70s to low 80s. Ensemble guidance signals at \ntransition to a more unsettled weather pattern Wednesday night \ninto late-week with chances for rain returning to the forecast.",
